WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
SQL injection can let attackers alter database queries that support payment records,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:09.300
customer accounts, orders, refunds, or administrative reporting.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.120
The impact can include data exfiltration, data corruption,

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.740
and unauthorized business actions.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.360
Command injection can occur when application input is passed into operating system

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.620
commands, diagnostic utilities, file converters, or administrative jobs.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.440
In payment environments,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.500
remote code execution can create a direct path to sensitive systems.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.160
NoSQL and LDAP injection follow the same pattern:

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.740
attacker-controlled data changes the meaning of a query or filter.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.720
APIs and identity integrations need the same careful input handling as traditional web

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.220
forms. Output encoding, input validation, and parameterized operations work together.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.400
Parameterization keeps data separate from instructions,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.180
validation rejects unexpected input,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.520
and encoding protects the context where data is displayed.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:05.260
Use parameterized queries and safe APIs. Validate input against strict expected formats.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.640
Encode output for the correct context and avoid leaking detailed errors.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.300
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:20.480
6.2 - Secure software development processes; Req. 6.4 - Change control; Req.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.980
11.3 - Application vulnerability testing.
